{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"alias":"quartzitic-deathful-needletail","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Context / Problem\n\n`tests/unit/test_mcp_gate_async.py::test_review_plan_start_returns_a_handle_fast_then_polls_to_passed` asserts a wall-clock bound: `review_plan_start` must return in under 2 s. On 2026-11-03 it failed locally at `assert 2.591... < 2` (\"async start must return a handle without blocking on the gate\") during a `pytest -n 4` run on a host at load average 29-38, with the gate stubbed. Run alone on the same host it passed. The bound was already widened once under `jeanlike-hick-azurevase`.\n\nA wall-clock threshold measures host load as well as whether the start call blocks on the gate, so it fails on a busy host without any regression.\n\n## Next check\n\nReplace the elapsed-time bound with an ordering signal: hold the stubbed gate body on an event that the test releases only after `review_plan_start` has returned, so a start that blocks on the gate deadlocks into a bounded wait (fail) and a non-blocking start returns regardless of host load.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [ ] The test proves \"start does not wait for the gate\" without an elapsed-time threshold.\n- [ ] The test fails when `review_plan_start` is changed to run the gate synchronously, and passes on main.","id":"2698-ff06-5f35-463c","parent_id":"","priority":3,"tags":["ops-sweep"],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"test_review_plan_start_returns_a_handle_fast times out under load"},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1793765133151987001,"uuid":"b1e0694d-a53d-4935-8f9c-b5547baafb7e"}